  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 AM Wednesday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Record or nearly so heat likely through Friday \n\nAn anomalously strong sub-tropical ridge centered over the lwr MS \nValley, observed in 00Z/14th 700 mb and 500 mb data at 321 dam and \n595 dam, respectively, will progress across and offshore the \nSoutheast through 12Z Fri. A series of mid-level disturbances, \nconvectively-generated and/or amplified from frontal convection from \nthe mid MS valley to the cntl and srn Plains, will progress around \nthe ridge in generally wly flow across the lwr OH Valley and Middle \nAtlantic through early Thu. Deep layer flow will then strengthen and \nback to swly ahead of a vigorous, positive tilt and increasingly-\nphased trough, comprised of a srn stream cyclone now over the srn \nUT/nrn AZ and a nrn stream shortwave trough over BC, forecast to \nreach the upr Great Lakes by 12Z Fri. \n\nAt the surface, a lee trough will develop and sharpen across VA and \nthe Carolinas today, while 1018-1019 mb high pressure now centered \nover MS/AL will progress across and offshore the Southeast. \nMeanwhile, a wavy front now stretching from a 1008 mb low along the \ncoast of ME, wwd across the nrn Middle Atlantic and lwr Great Lakes, \nthen swwd across the mid MS Valley and srn and cntl Plains, will \nprogress swd across the Middle Atlantic and reach the VA/NC border \nvicinity by 12Z Thu. The front should then drift swd into the far ne \nPiedmont and nrn Coastal Plain of NC on Thu, propelled by a ~1025 mb \nhigh (stronger than the preceding one over the Southeast) forecast \nto progress across the Great Lakes. The front will then retreat nwd \nacross the Middle Atlantic Thu night-Fri, ahead of a cyclone \nforecast to deepen considerably while tracking newd from the OH \nValley to the Canadian Maritimes (~20-25 mb from 00Z Fri to 12Z \nSat). \n\nUpr 80s to mid 90s temperatures that were present Tue across the \nGulf Coast states will expand newd and across the Carolinas today, \nbeneath and downstream of the anomalously strong ridge aloft. That \nheat will generally remain in a persistence pattern in the warm \nsector, equatorward of the aforementioned backdoor front that will \noffer cooling throughout VA and into the far ne Piedmont and nrn \nCoastal Plain of NC on Thu. However, mid and high-level debris \ncloudiness, from upstream convection and along the path of the \nconvectively-augmented disturbances, may keep temperatures from \nreaching their full sun heating potential on Thu, especially across \nnrn zones that may otherwise remain in the warm sector/ahead of the \nbackdoor front. Heat within the expanding and mostly sunny warm \nsector on Fri will be accompanied by a gusty swly surface wind, \nafter lighter winds Wed-Thu. \n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A strong cold front will sweep across central NC \nFriday evening, with a significant cooldown for the weekend and the \npotential for some patchy frost.\n\nA potent nrn stream s/w aloft will drop sewd across Ontario and the \nGreat Lakes Thu and Fri, deepening the trough over the ern US as it \ninteracts with another s/w ejecting enewd from the cntl Rockies to \nthe Great Lakes. The trough will continue to deepen as it moves ewd \nacross the eastern US and offshore Fri night/Sat. At the surface, an \narea of low pressure lifting newd across the mid-MS and OH Valley on \nThu will rapidly deepen as it continues across the Great Lakes and \nNortheast US Thu night/Fri. The trailing cold front will move across \ncentral NC late Fri/Fri night. There is still a chance for some \nprecipitation with the fropa, but amounts should be light and \nlimited in coverage. The low-level jet is expected to strengthen \nover the area Fri eve, with 30-40 kts at 925mb. The pressure \ngradient will also be quite strong as a ~1035 mb high over the \nPlains builds ewd. Expect strong, gusty winds with and behind the \nfront Fri eve through Fri night. The high may temporarily weaken \never so slightly as it builds ewd across the mid-MS and OH Valley \nregions on Sat, before settling over the mid-Atlantic Sat night/Sun, \nthen offshore by Mon.\n\nTemperatures should fall about 30 degrees from Friday aft to Sat \nmorning. By Saturday, highs will be similar to the lows from Thu \nnight (generally low to mid 60s). Lows should drop another 10 \ndegrees or so Sat night, as the high settles over the mid-Atlantic, \nwhen lows may drop into the upr 30s across the nrn Piedmont and \nupper 30s to mid 40s elsewhere across cntl NC. The usual cooler, \nsheltered areas may see lows in the mid 30s, with some patchy frost \npossible should the mid 30s lows be realized.",
